/** @page qore_modules Developing Qore Modules

    Copyright (C) 2003 - 2026 Qore Technologies, s.r.o.

    @section module_dev Qore Module Development

    Binary modules are shared libraries (<tt>.so</tt> / <tt>.dylib</tt> / <tt>.dll</tt>) that extend the %Qore language with new classes,
    functions, constants, and namespaces.

    Since %Qore 3.0, binary modules use Module API 2.2, which requires a single
    \c extern \c "C" module description function instead of the old-style exported global variables.

    @subsection module_api_version Module API Version

    A binary module carries the inline code and the type layouts of the %Qore headers that it was compiled with, so
    %Qore loads only a module built for the module API that it implements (@ref QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR and
    @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR, which the module sets in its description function).  A module file is named
    <tt>{name}-api-{major}.{minor}.qmod</tt> after the module API of the headers that it was built with, and a module
    built with the headers of another module API must be rebuilt:
    - looked up by its feature name (\c %%requires, @ref Qore::load_module() "load_module()"), only the files of the
      supported module API are considered, so the module is not found; the \c LOAD-MODULE-ERROR exception names each
      file of the feature built for another module API that is in the module path:
      <tt>...; found '/usr/lib/qore-modules/xml-api-2.1.qmod' built for module API 2.1: rebuild it for module API
      2.2</tt>
    - loaded by its path, it is refused with a \c LOAD-MODULE-ERROR exception that reports the API mismatch

    Module API 2.1 covers all public ABI changes made during %Qore 3.0 development after module API 2.0 was introduced
    (enum value ownership, new virtual methods in the I/O, iterator and polling classes, the representation of
    @ref QoreValue, program IDs, among others); a module built with the 2.0 headers is refused.  Module API 2.2 adds
    QoreModuleInfo::cmd_caller_aware and QoreModuleCommandContext, through which a module command handler judges the
    code that issues a command (see the parse command handler below); a module built with the 2.1 headers is refused.

    @note Any change to inline code, or to an exported or inline-visible type or function, in the public headers
    (<tt>include/qore</tt>, outside <tt>include/qore/intern</tt>) that a module compiled with the previous headers would
    get wrong increments @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR (or @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR for an incompatible redesign).

    @subsection module_header Module Description Function

    Every binary module must define exactly one \c extern \c "C" function named
    <tt>{module_name}_qore_module_desc</tt> that populates a @ref QoreModuleInfo struct.

    @note If the module name contains hyphens, they are converted to underscores when looking up the description
    function symbol.  For example, a module named \c "my-module" should define
    \c my_module_qore_module_desc(QoreModuleInfo&).

    @code{.cpp}
    extern "C" DLLEXPORT void mymodule_qore_module_desc(QoreModuleInfo& mod_info) {
        mod_info.name = "mymodule";
        mod_info.version = PACKAGE_VERSION;
        mod_info.desc = "My Qore module";
        mod_info.author = "Author Name <author@example.com>";
        mod_info.url = "http://example.com";
        mod_info.api_major = QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR;
        mod_info.api_minor = QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR;
        mod_info.init = mymodule_module_init;
        mod_info.ns_init = mymodule_module_ns_init;
        mod_info.del = mymodule_module_delete;
        mod_info.ns = &MyModuleNS;
        mod_info.license = QL_MIT;
        mod_info.license_str = "MIT";
    }
    @endcode

    The @ref QoreModuleInfo struct has the following fields:
    - \c name — the module's feature name; must be unique across all loaded modules
    - \c version — the module version string
    - \c desc — a human-readable description
    - \c author — the module author(s)
    - \c url — a URL for the module
    - \c api_major / \c api_minor — must be set to @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR and @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR
    - \c init — the module initialization callback (@ref qore_module_init_t)
    - \c ns_init — the namespace initialization callback (@ref qore_module_ns_init_t)
    - \c del — the module cleanup/destructor callback (@ref qore_module_delete_t)
    - \c parse_cmd — optional parse command handler (@ref qore_module_parse_cmd_t); set to \c nullptr if not used
    - \c cmd_caller_aware — set to \c true if the parse command handler honors the restrictions of the code that issues
      a command (QoreModuleCommandContext); a module that does not set it is not given commands from restricted code
    - \c license — the license type (default: @ref QL_MIT); set to @ref QL_GPL if the module links with GPL code
    - \c license_str — the license as a string (e.g. \c "MIT", \c "LGPL", \c "GPL")
    - \c dependencies — a list of other binary module names this module depends on (\c strvec_t)
    - \c info — an optional @ref QoreHashNode pointer with extra module metadata (default: \c nullptr)
    - \c ns — the module's namespace with static storage duration, if any (default: \c nullptr); it is purged when
      the module is deleted, see @ref qore_module_teardown (since %Qore 3.0, @ref QORE_HAS_MODULE_NS_PURGE)

    @note The @ref QoreModuleInfo struct uses default member initializers, so fields like \c license (defaults to
    @ref QL_MIT) and callback pointers (default to \c nullptr) only need to be set if non-default values are
    required.  This extensible design means new fields can be added with sensible defaults without breaking existing
    modules.

    @subsection module_functions Module Callback Functions

    @par Module Initialization — \c qore_module_init_t

    @code{.cpp}
    typedef void (*qore_module_init_t)(QoreModuleInitContext& ctx, ExceptionSink& xsink);
    @endcode

    Called when the module is first loaded.  Use this to create namespaces, register classes, constants, and functions.
    The @ref QoreModuleInitContext struct provides the \c path to the module file.  Report errors by raising
    exceptions on \c xsink; if any exception is raised, the module load will fail.

    @par Namespace Initialization — \c qore_module_ns_init_t

    @code{.cpp}
    typedef void (*qore_module_ns_init_t)(QoreNamespace* root_ns, QoreNamespace* qore_ns, ExceptionSink& xsink);
    @endcode

    Called when the module's namespace additions need to be provisioned to a @ref QoreProgram object — either when
    the module is first loaded or when a program requests the module's feature after it was already loaded by another
    program.  Typically this copies the module's reference namespace into the Qore namespace tree.  Report errors on
    \c xsink.

    @par Module Cleanup — \c qore_module_delete_t

    @code{.cpp}
    typedef void (*qore_module_delete_t)();
    @endcode

    Called when the module is unloaded (typically when the %Qore library shuts down).  Free any resources allocated
    during initialization, including every Qore value the module holds outside its namespace; see
    @ref qore_module_teardown.

    @par Parse Command Handler — \c qore_module_parse_cmd_t (optional)

    @code{.cpp}
    typedef void (*qore_module_parse_cmd_t)(const QoreString& cmd, ExceptionSink* xsink);
    @endcode

    If set, allows the module to handle parse-time commands issued via the \c %module-cmd directive, and the commands
    issued at run time with Program::issueModuleCmd(), QoreProgram::issueModuleCmd() and
    ModuleManager::issueRuntimeCmd(), as well as the commands that a compiled program records and replays when it is
    loaded.  Set to \c nullptr in the description function if not used (this is the default).

    @par Module Commands and the Restrictions of the Code that Issues Them (module API 2.2)

    The handler runs in the Program that the command applies to: \c getProgram() there is the target of the command,
    and its parse options say nothing about the code that issued the command.  A handler decides what a command may do
    from the restrictions of the issuing code, which Qore gives it in the QoreModuleCommandContext returned by
    QoreModuleCommandContext::get(), captured from the issuing code before Qore switches to the target Program:
    - the restricting parse options of the issuing code (QoreModuleCommandRestrictions::getParseOptions()), which are
      its effective run-time parse options, so also a \c %push-parse-options block; code of a library module (a module
      found in a module directory, not one that the code loading it chose by a path of its own) is governed only by
      the sandbox, as module code is everywhere in %Qore: the parse options of its Program are only what it inherited
      from the code that loaded it, so a command issued at run time by library module code has none, and neither has a
      \c %module-cmd directive in the source of a library module that is being loaded; a module that the loading code
      chose by a path of its own is judged by the restrictions of that code; for a \c %module-cmd directive in other
      code, the options in force at the directive (and those of the code that runs the parse); for a command replayed from a compiled program, the options of
      the code that loads the program together with the options that were in force at the directive when the program
      was compiled (the options that the program is declared with, for a program compiled before they were recorded),
      except for a library module that is loaded compiled, which has only the latter;
    - whether a sandbox governs the issuing code (QoreModuleCommandRestrictions::isSandboxed()); a sandbox that allows
      uncontrolled APIs (QoreSandboxManager::setUncontrolledApiPolicy()), which a host attaches to trusted code only
      for its interrupts or limits, does not make the code sandboxed;
    - how the command arrived (QoreModuleCommandContext::getOrigin()), and the issuing and target Programs.

    The restrictions only grow within a command: a command that a handler issues itself has at least the restrictions
    of the command it handles.  The context is valid only while the handler runs, on its thread: pass
    QoreModuleCommandContext::copy() to another thread.  Check every command that executes code, changes a classpath or
    a search path, touches the filesystem or the network, or changes state that all Programs share, and refuse it
    when the issuing code may not do that:

    @code{.cpp}
    static void mymodule_parse_cmd(const QoreString& cmd, ExceptionSink* xsink) {
        const QoreModuleCommandContext* ctx = QoreModuleCommandContext::get();
        assert(ctx);
        if (cmd == "exec") {
            // not for sandboxed code, nor for code that forbids uncontrolled APIs or embedded logic
            if (ctx->check(PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS | PO_NO_EMBEDDED_LOGIC, true, "MYMODULE-COMMAND-ERROR", "exec", xsink)) {
                return;
            }
            // ...
        }
    }
    @endcode

    A module whose handler checks the context for every such command sets \c QoreModuleInfo::cmd_caller_aware to
    \c true in its description function.  A module that does not set it is not given a command that restricted code
    issues (code that is sandboxed or has a parse option that restricts a functional domain): the command is refused
    with \c PARSE-COMMAND-ERROR or \c RUNTIME-COMMAND-ERROR before the handler runs.  Code that is not restricted can
    issue commands to every module.

    @warning The library creates the @ref QoreModuleInfo struct and validates the module API version only after the
    description function has filled it in, since the version is one of its fields: the version cannot be checked
    before the description function runs.  A struct created by a library that implements module API 2.1 or earlier
    has no \c cmd_caller_aware field, so a module built with module API 2.2 that such a library loads (by an explicit
    path or a file name without the API suffix; the library does not look for a \c -api-2.2.qmod file) would write
    past the end of the struct.  A module that may be loaded by such a library sets the field only if the library
    implements module API 2.2; the library then refuses the module for its API version, without the write:
    @code{.cpp}
    // the field exists only in a library that implements module API 2.2
    if (is_module_api_supported(2, 2)) {
        mod_info.cmd_caller_aware = true;
    }
    @endcode

    @note Through library module code, restrictions travel as they do everywhere in %Qore: a sandbox that governs the
    caller governs the module code that issues a command (QoreModuleCommandRestrictions::isSandboxed() is true, and a
    handler refuses what sandboxed code may not do), but a parse option that restricts the caller, or that the module's
    Program inherited from its loader (\c PO_NO_FILESYSTEM, for example), does not restrict the module code: a command
    to change a classpath that the code of a library module issues at run time is not refused for a caller that has
    only \c PO_NO_FILESYSTEM (see design/module-sandboxing-audit-guide.md).

    @par The Restrictions of the Caller of a Module Function (module API 2.2)

    A module function or method that decides what its caller may do (a database driver that judges a classpath
    option, for example) asks QoreModuleCommandRestrictions::getCallerRestrictions() instead of reading
    \c getProgram()->getParseOptions() or using QoreReflectionCaller.  It returns what a module command issued by
    the same code would have in its QoreModuleCommandContext: the restricting parse options of the calling code and
    whether a sandbox governs it.  Code of a library module has no parse options, only its sandbox, so a module
    that applies the rule that a command issued by library module code is judged by the sandbox only, applies it
    to its functions as well; the parse options of the Program of a library module are only what it inherited from
    the code that loaded it.

    @par Destructors of Builtin Classes of the Uncontrolled-API Domain

    A sandbox that refuses uncontrolled APIs refuses every call of a builtin function or method in the
    \c QDOM_UNCONTROLLED_API domain made for the code that it governs (\c INVALID-FUNCTION-ACCESS), with two
    exceptions.  The sandbox introspection and barrier methods \c SandboxManager::isGoverned(),
    \c SandboxManager::getGoverningPolicies() and \c SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy() are judged by the code
    that calls them, not by the whole call stack, so trusted code can call them for a sandboxed caller.  And the
    destructor of a builtin class is not refused, because the object is destroyed whenever its last reference is
    released (also at the end of a scope or when its %Program is deleted) and a refusal would only leave its resources
    unreleased.  A builtin destructor therefore runs for sandboxed code: it must only release what the object holds
    (memory, handles, references to foreign objects) and must not do I/O, open connections, or run code on behalf of
    the caller; work of that kind belongs in a method, which the sandbox judges.  Releasing a foreign object that the
    object holds can run the foreign runtime's own cleanup (a %Python \c __del__() method, a Java cleaner), which
    happens at release in any case.  See @ref sandbox_uncontrolled_api in the language reference.

    @par The Setter of a Datasource Option

    A DBI driver applies the options of a datasource when it opens a connection (Datasource::getConnectOptions()),
    which can happen on another thread and for other code than the code that set them: a DatasourcePool opens a
    connection on the thread that first needs one.  A driver that judges an option therefore asks
    Datasource::getOptionRestrictions() for the restrictions of the code that set it, and
    Datasource::getOptionPolicyManagerRef() for that code's filesystem policy, instead of
    QoreModuleCommandRestrictions::getCallerRestrictions(); it falls back to the caller only when no setter is
    recorded.  The restrictions are checked first, and an option that they refuse is refused whatever the policy is:
    Datasource::getOptionPolicyManagerRef() returns nullptr whenever no sandbox policy governed the setter, also for
    sandboxed code inside a trusted \c SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy() call, so a missing policy does not mean
    that the setter was trusted.  Only an option that the restrictions allow is checked against the policy:

    @code{.cpp}
static int my_set_option(Datasource* ds, const char* opt, const QoreValue val, ExceptionSink* xsink) {
#ifdef _QORE_HAS_DATASOURCE_OPTION_RESTRICTIONS
    QoreModuleCommandRestrictions setter;
    if (!ds->getOptionRestrictions(opt, setter)) {
        setter = QoreModuleCommandRestrictions::getCallerRestrictions();
    }
#else
    QoreModuleCommandRestrictions setter = QoreModuleCommandRestrictions::getCallerRestrictions();
#endif
    // first: the restrictions of the setter refuse the option regardless of its policy
    if (setter.check(PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS | PO_NO_FILESYSTEM, true, "MY-OPTION-ERROR", "set the option", xsink)) {
        return -1;
    }
#ifdef _QORE_HAS_DATASOURCE_OPTION_RESTRICTIONS
    // then: the files that the option names are checked against the setter's policy, if one governed it
    ReferenceHolder<QoreSandboxManager> policy(ds->getOptionPolicyManagerRef(opt), xsink);
    ...
#endif
    ...
}
    @endcode

    While a driver's option-setting function runs for \c Datasource::setOption() on an open connection, the new
    setter is pending: it is visible to the driver (\c Datasource::getOptionRestrictions()), but it is not yet stored,
    and copies of the datasource do not see it; it is stored with the value once the driver accepts the value.  An
    option that had no value before appears in \c Datasource::getConnectOptions() meanwhile as a placeholder entry
    with no value.  A connection that the driver opens meanwhile (a reconnect from the option-setting function) is
    not given the placeholder by the Qore library, but a driver's open function that reads
    \c Datasource::getConnectOptions() itself sees it: during an open the placeholder has no setter
    (\c Datasource::getOptionRestrictions() returns false for it), and the driver treats it as not set.  An option
    whose stored value is \c NOTHING has a setter and is a value like any other.  While a connection is opened, the
    setters that the driver reads are those of the stored values that the connection is given.  A driver must not
    call \c Datasource::setOption() from its own open or option-setting functions.

    See @ref sandbox_datasource_options in the language reference.

    @subsection module_example Complete Example

    The following is a minimal but complete example based on the \c astparser module:

    @code{.cpp}
    #include "qore/Qore.h"

    static void mymodule_module_init(QoreModuleInitContext& ctx, ExceptionSink& xsink);
    static void mymodule_module_ns_init(QoreNamespace* rns, QoreNamespace* qns, ExceptionSink& xsink);
    static void mymodule_module_delete();

    // Reference namespace — initialized once, copied for each QoreProgram
    static QoreNamespace MyModuleNS("Qore::mymodule");

    // Module description function — the entry point discovered by the Qore library
    extern "C" DLLEXPORT void mymodule_qore_module_desc(QoreModuleInfo& mod_info) {
        mod_info.name = "mymodule";
        mod_info.version = PACKAGE_VERSION;
        mod_info.desc = "Example Qore module";
        mod_info.author = "Author Name";
        mod_info.url = "http://example.com";
        mod_info.api_major = QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR;
        mod_info.api_minor = QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR;
        mod_info.init = mymodule_module_init;
        mod_info.ns_init = mymodule_module_ns_init;
        mod_info.del = mymodule_module_delete;
        // the namespace is purged when the module is deleted, before the types are freed
        mod_info.ns = &MyModuleNS;
        mod_info.license = QL_MIT;
        mod_info.license_str = "MIT";
    }

    static void mymodule_module_init(QoreModuleInitContext& ctx, ExceptionSink& xsink) {
        // Register classes, constants, and functions in the namespace
        MyModuleNS.addSystemClass(initMyClass(MyModuleNS));
        init_my_constants(MyModuleNS);
    }

    static void mymodule_module_ns_init(QoreNamespace* rns, QoreNamespace* qns, ExceptionSink& xsink) {
        // Copy the module's namespace into the Qore namespace
        qns->addNamespace(MyModuleNS.copy());
    }

    static void mymodule_module_delete() {
        // Free any resources allocated during initialization; MyModuleNS is purged after this returns
    }
    @endcode

    @note The module name (feature name) set via \c mod_info.name is used to uniquely identify the module.  If a
    module providing feature \c "widget" is already loaded, another module claiming the same feature cannot be loaded.

    @note The \c api_major and \c api_minor fields are used to verify that the module is compatible with the %Qore
    library loading it.  Always set these to @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MAJOR and @ref QORE_MODULE_API_MINOR.

    @note Classes must not be created more than once, because each Qore class gets a unique ID at creation time.
    Initialize classes in the \c init callback and use \c copy() in the \c ns_init callback.

    @subsection qore_module_teardown Releasing Module State at Shutdown

    A module releases all the %Qore state it owns when it is deleted: its namespace and the constants, classes,
    hashdecls and enums in it, and any %Qore value it keeps elsewhere (objects, call references, hashes, lists,
    strings, and Programs that the module created).  Modules are deleted by qore_cleanup(), modules that require
    another module before it, and all of this must be released then, before the library frees the types.  It must
    never be left to a C++ static destructor: static destructors run after qore_cleanup() has freed the types, when
    releasing a %Qore value (and running a destructor of a Qore object) is no longer safe, and they do not run at
    all when the process ends with \c _exit() (as it does when a background JIT compile is still running when the
    process exits, or in a child process that requested qore_fork_child_exit_quickly()), so a leak checker such as
    valgrind reports their values as lost.

    - a namespace with static storage duration is registered with QoreModuleInfo::ns in the description function;
      after the module's \c del function has run, it is purged with QoreNamespace::purge(), which releases
      everything in it and leaves it empty, so that its C++ static destructor has nothing left to release.  The
      \c del function can still use the namespace, and does not need to call QoreNamespace::clear() or
      QoreNamespace::purge() itself
    - everything else is released by the \c del function: a QoreProgram that the module owns is deleted there (with
      QoreProgram::waitForTerminationAndDeref()), and objects, call references and other values are dereferenced
      there
    - a module that also supports %Qore versions without QoreModuleInfo::ns tests @ref QORE_HAS_MODULE_NS_PURGE and
      calls QoreNamespace::clear() in its \c del function otherwise

    @code{.cpp}
    static QoreNamespace MyModuleNS("Qore::mymodule");
    static ResolvedCallReferenceNode* my_callback = nullptr;

    extern "C" DLLEXPORT void mymodule_qore_module_desc(QoreModuleInfo& mod_info) {
        // ...
        mod_info.del = mymodule_module_delete;
    #ifdef QORE_HAS_MODULE_NS_PURGE
        mod_info.ns = &MyModuleNS;
    #endif
    }

    static void mymodule_module_delete() {
        if (my_callback) {
            ExceptionSink xsink;
            my_callback->deref(&xsink);
            my_callback = nullptr;
        }
    #ifndef QORE_HAS_MODULE_NS_PURGE
        MyModuleNS.clear(nullptr);
    #endif
    }
    @endcode

    Debug builds of the library count the module namespaces that still hold values when the types are freed;
    with the \c QORE_TEARDOWN_REPORT environment variable set, the count and each such namespace are printed on
    \c stderr, and a registered namespace destroyed with content after that point fails an assertion.

    @subsection module_cpp_api_dev Sharing a C++ API Between Modules

    A module can publish a C++ API for other binary modules to consume, without the API having to be moved into
    libqore.  The exporting module declares a struct of function pointers in an installed header and publishes it
    through a single \c extern \c "C" entry point; the consuming module includes only that header and resolves the
    struct at run time with @ref q_get_module_cpp_api(), which loads the exporting module on demand and verifies that
    the version it publishes can serve the version the consumer was compiled against.

    A version mismatch between two independently released modules is therefore a %Qore-language exception at
    resolution time rather than a process abort at the first call, and an optional C++ dependency becomes possible —
    the C++ equivalent of the \c %%try-module directive.

    See @ref module_cpp_api for the entry point naming convention, the append-only and public-ABI-types-only rules,
    the process for bumping the major version, and complete producer and consumer examples.  Two builtin modules
    publish C++ APIs this way and are worth reading as real examples of both sides: @ref json_cpp_api, and
    @ref avro_cpp_api, which is itself a consumer of the first.

    @subsection module_environment Accessing the Process Environment

    The C library's environment functions are not safe to call from several threads at once while one of them
    changes the environment: \c setenv() may free the value it replaces (musl's does, so two threads replacing the
    same variable free the same memory twice and corrupt the heap) and may reallocate the \c environ array (every
    C library), so a thread calling \c getenv() or iterating \c environ at the same time reads freed memory.  %Qore
    serializes every access to the environment that it makes, including the Qore-language functions
    \c getenv(), \c setenv() and \c unsetenv() and the copy of the environment that a new process gets, with one
    process-wide read-write lock.  A module must use the
    same lock wherever it touches the environment, so never call \c getenv(), \c setenv(), \c unsetenv() or
    \c putenv() directly in a module:

    - to test whether a variable is set: SystemEnvironment::isSet()
    - to read a variable: QoreEnvironmentValue (a copy of the value), SystemEnvironment::get() or
      SystemEnvironment::getAsStringNode()
    - to change a variable: SystemEnvironment::set() or SystemEnvironment::unset()
    - to read and change several variables atomically: AtomicEnvironmentSetter, which holds the locks exclusively
      for its lifetime; the C library functions may be called directly while it exists
    - to read the environment with the C library directly (iterate \c environ, pass \c environ to \c posix_spawn()
      or \c execve(), or call a third-party function that reads the environment): QoreEnvironmentReadLock, which
      excludes changes to the environment for its lifetime

    @code{.cpp}
// a setting read from the environment: the value is copied under the lock
QoreEnvironmentValue max_conn("MYMOD_MAX_CONNECTIONS");
int max = max_conn ? atoi(max_conn.c_str()) : 10;

// a child process gets a consistent copy of the environment
{
    QoreEnvironmentReadLock env_lock;
    rc = posix_spawn(&pid, path, &actions, &attr, argv, environ);
}

// a third-party library that reads the environment and may load libraries: changes wait, fork() does not
{
    QoreEnvironmentReadLock env_lock;
    rc = third_party_init(&handle);
}

// a third-party function that only changes the environment: no other Qore thread uses the environment meanwhile
{
    AtomicEnvironmentSetter env_lock;
    third_party_set_env_defaults();
}
    @endcode

    The locks can be nested in one thread, but a thread holding a QoreEnvironmentReadLock cannot change the
    environment: SystemEnvironment::set() and SystemEnvironment::unset() return -1 with \c errno \c EDEADLK.
    Hold them only as long as necessary, since changes to the environment wait until they are released.

    Before \c fork(), the library's fork handlers wait until no other thread is changing the environment or copying
    a variable (so the child gets a consistent copy of the environment and starts with the locks released); they do
    not wait for the holders of a QoreEnvironmentReadLock, which may therefore be held across calls that take a long
    time, wait for other threads or load or unload libraries, such as a resolver library that calls \c dlopen() and
    \c dlclose() (on macOS, \c fork() holds the lock of the dynamic linker while the fork handlers run).  An
    AtomicEnvironmentSetter is waited for: while it exists, only read and change the environment, and do not call
    \c fork().  \c posix_spawn() runs no fork handlers, so hold a QoreEnvironmentReadLock while passing \c environ
    to it, as above.

    @note The lock only covers code that takes it.  A third-party library or an embedded language runtime that
    reads the environment internally (for example a resolver or TLS library reading its configuration variables, or
    a JavaScript, Python or Java runtime reading or changing \c environ) is not serialized with %Qore.  Where the
    module calls such a function, hold a QoreEnvironmentReadLock (or an AtomicEnvironmentSetter if the function
    only changes the environment) around it; otherwise the module must not let
    that code change the environment while other threads may use it, and should pass values to it some other way
    than through the environment.

    @subsection module_program_external_data Data of a Module in a Program

    A module keeps its data for a @ref Qore::Program "Program" as external data of the Program
    (QoreProgram::setExternalData(), QoreProgram::getExternalData() and QoreProgram::removeExternalData(), with a
    subclass of AbstractQoreProgramExternalData).  When a Program is created from a parent Program that has external
    data, the child gets its own data from AbstractQoreProgramExternalData::copy(), called on the data of the parent
    once the child is set up, and then from AbstractQoreProgramExternalData::init() on the child's data.

    - \c copy() runs without the lock of either Program: it can take the module's own locks and call the %Qore API,
      including that of the parent Program, while other threads of the parent use the module.  It can run in several
      threads at the same time for the same data of the parent, once for each child being created, so it only reads
      that data, or synchronizes its changes itself.
    - The data of the parent stays valid while it is copied: QoreProgram::removeExternalData() on the parent waits
      until the copies have been made, so a module must not call it while it holds a lock that its \c copy() takes,
      nor from its \c copy().
    - Before %Qore 3.0, \c copy() was called with the parent Program's lock held, and a thread of the module that
      took the Program's lock while it held the module's lock (to parse, for example) could deadlock with a thread
      creating a child Program.

    @code{.cpp}
// the data of the module in a Program, created by the module's ns_init() function
class MyModProgramData : public AbstractQoreProgramExternalData {
public:
    MyModProgramData(QoreProgram* pgm) : pgm(pgm) {
    }

    // a child Program gets its own data; the module's lock can be taken here
    AbstractQoreProgramExternalData* copy(QoreProgram* child) const override {
        AutoLocker al(mymod_lock);
        return new MyModProgramData(child);
    }

    void doDeref() override {
        delete this;
    }

private:
    QoreProgram* pgm;
};
    @endcode

    @subsection qore_module_fork_handlers Process-Wide Locks and fork()

    A child process created with \c fork() runs only the thread that called \c fork(): a lock that another thread
    held at that moment stays held in the child forever, and the child waits forever the first time it needs it.
    %Qore therefore runs fork handlers for its own process-wide locks for every \c fork() in the process, whoever
    calls it: before \c fork() they acquire the locks in one documented order (their ranks), so that no other thread
    holds them while the process is copied, and after it they release them in the parent and initialize them again
    in the child.

    A module with a process-wide lock (a static lock or a lock of a static object, not the lock of an object that
    Qore code creates) registers fork handlers for it with qore_register_fork_handler() in its initialization
    function and removes them with qore_unregister_fork_handler() in its delete function (not while static objects
    are constructed or destroyed, when the dynamic loader holds its lock).  The rank places the lock in the order of
    the library's locks (see @ref QoreForkRank):
    - @ref QFR_MODULE_LEAF "QFR_MODULE_LEAF": the lock's holders take no lock but the library's leaf locks (they only allocate memory
      and work with strings and containers)
    - @ref QFR_MODULE_CANCELLABLE "QFR_MODULE_CANCELLABLE": the lock is taken while no other registered lock is held, and its holders can check
      for cancellation (qore_check_cancel()) or raise exceptions, but do not start threads or wait for them
    - @ref QFR_MODULE_RESET "QFR_MODULE_RESET": the lock is held while user code runs or while a thread waits for input, so \c fork()
      must not wait for it: register only a child handler, which initializes the lock again; if a thread can call
      \c fork() while it holds the lock (for example from a callback), the module counts its holds per thread and
      the child handler takes the lock again as often as the forking thread held it

    For a QoreThreadLock, qore_fork_prepare_thread_lock(), qore_fork_parent_thread_lock() and
    qore_fork_child_thread_lock() are the handlers; a QoreRecursiveThreadLock is initialized again with
    qore_fork_reinit_recursive_thread_lock().

    @code{.cpp}
// a registry of the module whose lock's holders check for cancellation
static QoreThreadLock registry_lock;
static QoreForkHandlerRegistration* registry_fork_reg = nullptr;

static void mymod_module_init(QoreModuleInitContext& ctx, ExceptionSink& xsink) {
    registry_fork_reg = qore_register_fork_handler(QFR_MODULE_CANCELLABLE, "mymod_registry",
        qore_fork_prepare_thread_lock, qore_fork_parent_thread_lock, qore_fork_child_thread_lock, &registry_lock);
    if (!registry_fork_reg) {
        xsink.outOfMemory();
        return;
    }
    // ...
}

static void mymod_module_delete() {
    qore_unregister_fork_handler(registry_fork_reg);
    // ...
}
    @endcode

    The handlers run in the thread that calls \c fork(): they must not throw, register or remove fork handlers,
    acquire any lock but their own, or allocate memory in the child.  Code that calls \c fork() must not hold a
    lock that a prepare handler acquires (it would wait for itself) nor the environment lock (see
    @ref module_environment).

    @subsection qore_module_fork_exit Exit-Time Code of Third-Party Libraries in a Child Process

    A child process created with \c fork() exits with \c exit(), which runs the \c atexit() handlers and the C++
    static destructors of every library in the process.  Exit-time code of a third-party library that assumes the
    process that loaded it is not safe in a child: it hangs the child when it joins the library's worker threads
    (they exist only in the parent) or takes a lock that one of those threads held when the process was copied, and
    it aborts the child when it destroys a kernel object that the child does not inherit (such as a Mach port on
    macOS).  The module cannot change that code, so it calls qore_fork_child_exit_quickly() in its child fork
    handler: the child then ends with \c _exit() after %Qore's own cleanup, and no exit-time handler runs.  Any number
    of modules can call it, from handlers of any rank; one call is enough, and further calls have no effect.

    @code{.cpp}
// the exit-time code of the library joins its worker threads and takes its global lock
static void mymod_fork_child(void* arg) {
    qore_fork_child_exit_quickly();
}

static void mymod_module_init(QoreModuleInitContext& ctx, ExceptionSink& xsink) {
    mymod_exit_reg = qore_register_fork_handler(QFR_MODULE_LEAF, "mymod_exit", nullptr, nullptr, mymod_fork_child,
        nullptr);
    if (!mymod_exit_reg) {
        xsink.outOfMemory();
        return;
    }
    // ...
}
    @endcode

    In a child in which a module requested it, the exits made through %Qore are:
    - the end of the main program of the \c qore and \c qr programs and of executables compiled with \c qcc,
      including one with an exception that was not caught (exit status 3; 1 in an executable that \c qcc links from
      script objects): the program and its threads are torn down and the delete functions of the modules run, as in
      any process; then the standard I/O streams are flushed and the process ends with \c _exit() and the
      program's exit status.  An embedding program that returns from \c main() calls qore_fork_child_quick_exit()
      with its exit status after qore_cleanup()
    - @ref Qore::exit() "exit()" and qore_exit_process() (also used for a @ref Qore::GetOpt "GetOpt" error): as in
      any process, no program is torn down and no module delete function runs; the library's helper threads are
      stopped, and if only the calling thread is left, the standard I/O streams are flushed and the process ends with
      \c _exit() and the given status instead of \c exit().  With other threads running, the process ends with
      \c _Exit() and the given status, without flushing, as in any process
    - a \c SIGTERM with no %Qore handler ends the process with \c _Exit() and status 143, as in any process

    The exit status is the one the process would have had.  Termination by a signal, \c abort() and \c _exit() are
    not affected.  The request is never in effect in the parent: it only takes effect when made from a child handler
    of a \c fork(), and only in the child created by that \c fork(); a child that this child creates with \c fork()
    exits quickly only if a module requests it again in that \c fork(), and a program started with
    @ref Qore::exec() "exec()" starts without it.

    Since no exit-time handler of any library or module runs in such a child, a module must not rely on an
    \c atexit() handler or a static destructor for cleanup that has to happen in a child process: it does it in its
    delete function (which runs at the end of the main program) or in its child fork handler.
 */
